==French==

===Etymology 1===
[[re-#French|re-]] + [[porter#French|porter]]

====Pronunciation====
* {{audio|Fr-reporter.ogg|audio}}
* {{IPA|/ʁǝ.pɔʁ.te/|lang=fr}}

====Verb====
{{fr-verb}}

# To [[carry]] back to where it something was.
# To [[wear]] again.
# To [[transfer]] {{qualifier|an emotion}}.
#: '''''Reporter''' son amour sur quelqu'un'' — To transfer one's love to somebody
# To [[put back]]; to [[put off]]; to [[postpone]].
#: ''Après avoir été '''reportées''' de nombreuses fois, les épreuves ont finalement eu lieu.''
#* {{quote-book|year=1999|author=[[w:Chrystine Brouillet|Chrystine Brouillet]]|title=Les Fiancées de l'Enfer|isbn=2-89021-363-3| page=116| passage=''Graham avait '''reporté''' la fête, suggérant d'attendre la find de l'enquête pour s'amuser.'' — Graham had put back the party, suggesting that they wait until the investigation was over to have fun.}}
# {{usually|lang=fr|impersonal}} To [[carry]] or [[take]] back in time.
#: ''Il faut se '''reporter''' au mois dernier pour comprendre.'' — We have to go back in time amonth to understand.
# {{pronominal|lang=fr}} To [[refer]]; to [[check]].
#: ''Pour mieux saisir le problème, on se '''reportera''' au chapitre 4.'' — To better understand the issue, the reader is referred to chapter 4.
# {{maths|and|finance|lang=fr}} To [[carry]] over or forward.
#: ''Cet idiot avait oublié de '''reporter''' un 3!'' — That idiot forgot to carry over a 3!

=====Conjugation=====
{{fr-conj-er|report|avoir}}

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|en|fr}} [[#Noun|reporter]].

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/ʁǝpɔʁtɛʁ/|lang=fr}}

====Noun====
{{fr-noun|m}}

# A news [[#Noun|reporter]]; a [[journalist]].

=====Usage notes=====
* The word is much less frequent in [[Quebec]], where it is seen as an [[anglicism]], words like {{term|journaliste}}, {{term|correspondant}} or {{term|envoyé}} are preferred.

====See also====
* [[reporteur]]

